        <Title>Before turning off seduced</Title>
        <Text>“That’s it!” Vigram shouted. Below, in the leech lake, two leechmen tried to force the ox’s snout into a bag of freeweed smoke. “That’s IT!”
Vigram rushed to the edge of the patio. His eyes flew wide when the yellow-headed ox bucked both leechmen off its back. With another hip-shift and buck, the ox broke its constraints, then jumped its rail. The beast tore through the leech lake, kicking up waves of confused, copulating leeches.
“Stop that ox!” Vigram shouted as he pointed with his loaded brush. “There’s three silver-strings of leeches attached to that beast! If you don’t—”
“Vigram,” Lora moaned from her seat. “You’re rich already. You don’t need the money from another bundle of leeches. Let it go.”
“Don’t ask me to let go of my principles,” Vigram replied. “That beast is MINE!”
Vigram stomped back towards the canvas. He set his brush down on the palette, not caring that it rolled into a swatch of blue. Ever the man of action, he marched down to the leech lakes to slap his lazy leechmen. He ordered them after the ox. He screamed at them, bald head rumpling, but his screaming only worked against him, for it stirred a few bleeding cows and goats out of their drug induced reverie. Their horrid shrieks woke up others, and they shrieked too, a cascade of tortured screams. Vigram threw his head back and yelled right back at them. 
“He’ll be better for it,” Lora said as she slid off her stool. “He has to let it out once in a while.”
“You say you’re his niece?” Mandri asked.
Lora smiled as she sauntered over to the easel. Her intrepid hand brushed the back of Mandri’s arm. “I don’t like him either, if that’s what you’re wondering. Now, let me see what he has—oh wow…” 
Her portrait looked like her face had been painted onto an apple, then run over by a cartwheel.
“It could be better,” Mandri said. “I think it’s the hands.” He stepped forward to make a play at correcting them.
Lora sidled forward with him, stifling a laugh. “He’s turned me into a demon!” she said. “It’s not only the hands…though they do look like claws.” She grabbed Mandri’s own hand and gave it a squeeze. “Artists need strong and dexterous hands, don’t they?” She smiled, tried to catch him staring.
“Uh…no…” Mandri stammered. “Well, yes, but, uh… I don’t have any, uh, dexterity. I actually haven’t painted anything in years.”
“Why not?”
Mandri tore himself away from her grip. “It’s too expensive,” he said instinctively. “Look at all this pigment. The canvas. It would take me weeks to afford all of this. Your uncle makes this in half a finger.” He scraped a slap of yellow ochre back into its little clay jar. There was enough untouched pigment left on the palette for at least four more of these awkward lessons.
“Mandri, listen to me,” Lora said, demanding, causing him to fumble with the jar of paint. “Vigram doesn’t care about these lessons. The only reason you are here is so that I can get to know you, so stop avoiding me.”
“I’m not avoiding you it’s just—wait, why would you want to get to know me?”
“Because I’m looking for someone, and I think there’s good chance that someone is you.”
“What do you need me for?”
Lora , and said, “Why do you think a girl would need a boy, Mandri?”
She batted her eyes, tried to catch him staring. Mandri gulped.
“Lora, you’re a beautiful girl, but I’m sorry, I’m not available.”
“Yes you are.”
“No, you don’t understand. I don’t want anything to do with anyone.”
“Good,” Lora said. “I find that attractive.” An easy smile played on her lips. or the blunt demeanor, was a sudden ; Mandri no longer saw a girl trained in the art of woman-standing-still, he saw a person. 
“?” he asked, genuinely worried that this girl was .
“I am attracted to resentment,” Lora said. “When someone looks a certain way, unkempt, it’s irresistible. The more a man resents himself and his place in the world, the more likely I am to notice him. Does that sound like you?”
Mandri didn’t think of himself as unkempt. He hadn’t shaved in weeks and his robes might be fraying at the sleeves, but, well, anyways, she didn’t know a damned thing about him.
“I don’t understand what’s happening here.”
“Vigram said you were, ‘a thin veneer lidding a black pit of shame.’ He said you would attack me before confront your own shame.”
“First resentment, now its shame?”
“Yes. Exactly my type.”
“That’s what you look for in a man? Shame?”
“It is. Shame; sadness; exhaustion. The more the better.”
Lora picked up Vigram’s paintbrush and rolled it’s handle between her fingers. Her sly, expectant look pricked Mandri’s skin. She asked, “Have you ever been seduced?”
“Is that what’s happening here?”
“I’ll be more direct: are you still tethered to your flesh?”
“What?”
“I suppose I’ll be even more direct.” She stepped forward, into Mandri’s comfort zone, and closer still. Animal fear, kissing distance. She was about an eyebrow shorter than him, but a whole lot thicker. 
“Touch me,” she said.
“Why?” Mandri mumbled.
Lora laughed and grabbed Mandri’s arm to pull him into a squeeze. She caught his eyes, forced him to confront her lilac scent, her pounding heat, her smooth skin, her honeyed, aching lips.
“Lora…” Mandri muttered. He knew he had to be very careful here. “You’re a beautiful girl, but—”
“But what? You go in for men?”
“No, I just don’t understand what is going on here.”
“Have you never had fun before? You should try, I think it would be good for you.”
“And what do you get out of it?”
Lora’s inviting smile widened. Her warmth overwhelmed. The soft shadows of the vines overhead framed her hazel eyes with the most magnificent golden light—a magical vesture indeed.
She leaned in.
Her gentle lips parted just shy of his cheek.
“I’ll get the end of the world,” she said.
Her breath prickled his skin. And now he was on the precipice. His hand gripped her waist, ostensibly to push her away, but her brown robe was so very thin, and the heat of her, intense. Coupled with the curve of her figure, it was almost enough to make him forget that this was a total stranger who, by natural law, should find him repulsive. And more than that, this was not what he was here for.
A loud grunt drew his gaze to the trellis behind Lora’s flower-studded curls. In between the diamond gaps of wood and leafy vine, he glimpsed that giant yellow-headed ox hurtling towards them both. The lower half of its body was covered in swinging leeches.
“Watch out!”
He shoved Lora out of the way.
He dove aside himself.
The old ox, sagging spine and all, rammed right through the trellis, sending a shower of wood splinters onto the patio. Dewlap flapping, it crashed into Vigram’s easel. Several foot long leeches flew through the air.
“MRRRRUUUUUUUUUU!”
The ox’s wheeling hooves scratched and slipped on the brick. After a moment of chaos, it steadied itself, legs wide like a water bug, and then it bucked into the opposite side of the trellis. It broke through, scraped a few more leeches off its haunches, then disappeared into the marshgrass.

Vigram stormed back out onto the patio. His head was red and sweaty. His chest, panting. His eyes darted between Lora, who lay sprawled on the floor, Mandri, who was much the same, and his abandoned painting, which lay face down on the dirty brick. Two little leeches wiggled on top of the frame. Then his wild gaze seized upon the ox’s shaved rump as it bounced through the grass. His jaw dropped. His eyes widened. He transcended the bounds of anger, peaking into pure amazement, like he’d just heard an upstart beggar claim to be god, only to find out, the rascal was.

“You tempted him? I missed it?”
“We were interrupted.”
“I’m apologize. Deeply. Agh, the ox was,
“Vigram, calm yourself.”

“I see Fate!” Vigram declared.
Mandri brushed the dirt off the skirt of his robe. He stood up. “I’m leaving,” he said firmly. He was still bewildered by Lora’s advance, and the ox’s intrusion, and now, Vigram’s rapturous mood. Against all this, he dared to hold out his hand, upturned—“If you could pay me my money, that would be great.”

“Oh, calm yourself,” Lora said. All the girlish charm in her voice had vanished. &quot;Not every interruption is a scheme of Fate.” Stern and measured, she stood up and fixed her hair. She smiled at Mandri’s demanding hand like it was cute. “He is not as empty inside as you claimed,” she said. “Not only did he succumb to temptation, but he desired to protect himself from injury. He desires life.”
“What, because he dodged the ox’s charge? I addressed this before—we should not mistake any acts of self-preservation for vitality. He is a broken young man, I assure you.”
“I’m sorry,” Mandri interjected, still holding out his hand. “What is happening here?”
Vigram chuckled, then stroked his beard. “She was a test to determine how tethered you were to your flesh.”
“You were seduced,” Lora added.
“Seduced?” Mandri scoffed. “Is that what you were trying to do? I didn’t notice. You should try stuffing your tits.”
“You see!?” Vigram exclaimed. “The moment he learns your interest in him was a ruse, he moves to mock you. He cannot bear the thought that he was taken advantage of, made a fool. See the way he stands. I should say that the boy is nothing more than a thin veneer lidding a black pit of shame. I say, he is exactly what we need! Look at him! He is empty!”
“He is common,” Lora intoned. 
“But the ox! You never truly kissed!”
Lora gave Vigram a considerate frown. “I suppose it could be called an auspicious moment.” She eyed Mandri as though appraising a slave. “I shall query his Fate again.” She stomped back across the patio, then sort of drifted into the darkness of Vigram’s house.
Mandri’s legs were sweating fiercely now. He knew he should run, if only because these people were taking up far too much of his time with some nonsense. “Please,” he said. “Just pay me what I’m owed, and I’ll be on my way.”
“Mandri,” Vigram said, “What precisely do you believe you are owed?”
“A quarter silver string?”
“You are owed quite a bit more than that.”
Lora’s commanding voice boomed out of the house. “Tomorrow!” she said. “If you recall, I am required elsewhere to-day!”
“Ah,” Vigram said. He returned his attention to Mandri. “Tomorrow, then.” He reached into his robe’s pocket. He pulled out a quarter string of silver, then dropped it into Mandri’s cupped hand. “I want to see you here tomorrow morning. I can promise you an opportunity which will give you everything that you ever wanted. I should add, you will not have to work for it.”
“Sure,” Mandri mumbled as he fingered the short stack of silver coins. He did not count them, just felt their weight. He wasn’t interested in lingering any longer than he needed to. He wasn’t interested in returning any time soon. Definitely not tomorrow. He had plans for tomorrow.
He hurried down the patio’s steps, refusing to return Vigram’s parting nod.
“Look happier!” Vigram called. “You should be excited!”
Mandri gave a polite smile over his shoulder, then hurried down Vigram’s lane, which was the most terrifying lane because it was sandwiched between two leech lakes.
Mandri kept to the right. The lake on the left held leeches bigger than his arm. Instead of stalls penning in live animals, its banks were lined with bones. The lake’s center was a constant swirl as hundreds of leeches swarmed just under the water’s surface, bunch-crawling like caterpillars, slithering like eels, desperate to get in a taste of whatever corpse had bothered to drop in. Mandri grimaced at the sight of it. Then, suddenly, a round shape popped out of that writhing mass like a buoy. It rotated around, revealing itself to be a severed head, somewhat fresh, a human head, complete with close cropped hair, an upturned nose, and an open mouth which could not scream. A leech latched onto its cheek, and as soon as that had come, it sank.
“Nope!” Mandri said to himself. “Nope, nope, nope,” he repeated as he darted towards Vigram’s gate. Once onto the road, he quickened his pace. He didn’t feel out of reach until he crested the big hill that overlooked Vigram’s estate. Only then did he risk a look back.
Vigram, still on his patio, stared straight through the distance, right at Mandri. The burly man cupped his voice and shouted, “Fate, boy! Fate!”
Mandri pretended not to hear.
He walked all the way to Geem’s and used his pay to buy half a bushel of burgeons. They were down-valley burgeons, decent stuff, but the waxy capsule leaves were liable to split. The insides would dry out before he could get to them in the ways that he wanted to. But, that was all Geem had, so he bought them.
He continued along the road, stomping his way through the muddy wheel-ruts, slapping away the flies, humming nursery rhymes, trying anything to distract himself from overthinking the sight of that severed head. Because it was only a severed head. That’s all it was! It might have never belonged to a body, or been a person. There’s no way of knowing, really. Anything was possible. Besides, a rich man like Vigram was sure to accumulate enemies alongside the rest of his possessions. What was one little head?
Mandri thought about his barren farm.
He thought about relaxing under his tree.
He thought about sipping a milky hooch spiked through with burgeon juice.
He would like to escape into that hooch. It would be bliss.


