“Vigram,” Lora moaned. “Your assurances are always cheap. There is only what he is, and what he is not.”

“This all depresses me,” Vigram said as he returned to his painting. His charcoal under drawing was bleeding pus. “I require a break. You two…why don’t you two clean up for now.” He wiped his charcoal-stained fingers on an untouched corner of the canvas, then plodded off.


“Vigram, you can’t stop now,” Mandri said after him. “If you give up in the middle of a failing work, you’ll make a bad habit.”
“I know,” Vigram said. “I’ll return. You two…you two get along.”
Vigram winked at Lora, then smiled wide. “I’m going to go pick out some wine. Something with a sweet bouquet.” As soon as he announced he was leaving, he was gone.


