“Vigram!” she shouted as she pushed him away. 
Vigram stormed back out onto the patio, wine bottle in hand.  His eyes darted between Lora Mandri, puzzling out the reason for the awkward distance between them.
Lora side-eyed Vigram with contempt. “He failed,” she said curtly.
Vigram’s shoulders sank. “That’s not possible,” he argued. “Look at him!” He pointed his wine bottle at Mandri. “He’s as empty inside as any young man we’ll ever find. What did he do? Did he kiss you? That could mean anything!”
“He was seduced, Vigram. You know what that means.”
“It could mean a number of things!”
“I’m sorry,” Mandri interjected. “What is happening?”
Both Lora and Vigram stared at him as though they’d just heard a rock speak.
“Mandri, do you feel something for this girl?”
“I feel confused.”
“You see!” Vigram exclaimed. “Sheer confusion is explanation enough. It might have been a confused reflex, an instinct. You ended the test too soon.”
“You doubt me?”
Lump in Vigram’s throat. “No,” he said. “No, of course not. I’m only upset I have to keep looking.”
“Alright, if you could pay me, I’m going to go ahead and leave.”
“I’m very sorry, Mandri. I’m sorry. You’re not what we’re looking for.
“You’re going to stiff me?”
“In a manner of speaking, yes.”


Mandri got to his feet and brushed the dirt off the skirt of his robe. “I’m leaving,” he said.
“You are not allowed to leave!” Vigram shouted. “We’re not finished!”
“Let the boy go,” Lora sighed.
“Let him go? But we aren’t finished.”
“I think that fate has decided that we are. This was not meant to be.”
“Fate?” Vigram asked, suddenly forgetting his rage. “You mean to say…”
“I do,” Lora answered. She eyed Vigram coldly, as if to shush him up. “This was not meant to be. Pay the boy his money.”
“But—”
“Hold on,” Mandri said. “Was all of this an attempt to get me and her together?”
“Pay him,” Lora repeated.
“And why does that need to be said?” Mandri wondered. “Were you were going to stiff me?”
Vigram reached into his robe’s pocket. “In a manner of speaking, yes. But, things being as they are, here is your payment.” He pulled out a quarter string of silver, then dropped it into Mandri’s cupped hand. “Until we meet again.”


